<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uraniborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uraniborg was the observatory and residence built by the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> on the island of Ven in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a>, from 1576. Designed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace with instruments, a library, workshops and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, it was supplemented by the underground observatory of Stjerneborg. It was abandoned after Tycho left in 1597 and later demolished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uraniborg.jpg" alt="Uraniborg" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stanlekub · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uraniborgskiss_45.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Uraniborg, the Castle of Urania, was named for the Muse of astronomy. It served as an observatory and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a> laboratory and was the first custom-built observatory in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the last built without a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> as its main instrument. The cornerstone was laid on 8 August 1576, and the Flemish Renaissance building of brick framed in sandstone and limestone was built between 1576 and 1580 under the architect Hans van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a> and the sculptor Johan Gregor van der Schardt. The main building was square, about 15 metres on a side, with a semi-circular tower on its north and south sides. A planned wall was never built; an earth mound took its place and is the only remnant still in place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The main floor held four rooms, one for Brahe's family and three for visiting astronomers. The northern tower housed the kitchens and the southern a library, where Brahe etched the motto Non haberi sed esse. The upper floor had a large room for royal visitors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a>, who came on 20 March 1590, and the primary instruments stood in the towers and on balconies. A loft held eight small rooms for students, and the basement had an alchemical laboratory and a small prison room. Gardens supplied herbs for his medicinal experiments, and the gatehouses held his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> workshop. Ponds and a paper mill lay beyond the walls.</p><p>Shortly after construction it became clear that wind moved the tower-mounted instruments, so Brahe built Stjärneborg nearby, with its instruments in pits at ground level. Among his instruments were a brass azimuthal quadrant of 1576, a Great Globe begun in 1580 that carried over 1,000 stars by 1595, a Triangular Sextant of 1582 and a Great Equatorial Armillary of 1585. Observations at their best were accurate to about a minute of arc, ten times better than earlier data.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Work at Uraniborg included the observation of the comet of 1577 and led to Brahe's geo-heliocentric Tychonic system of 1588. He described the instruments in his 1598 book Astronomiae instauratae mechanica. The building was one of the first observatories funded entirely by a government for research, and it is estimated to have cost about 1 percent of the state budget during construction.</p><p>Brahe left Ven in 1597 after losing the favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>, and the buildings were destroyed after his death in 1601. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> published his former master's star catalogue in 1627. The Round Tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, inaugurated in 1642, replaced Uraniborg's astronomical role. Jean Picard visited the ruins in 1671 to record the site's position. Excavations in the 1950s revealed parts of Stjärneborg, the gardens have been replanted since 1985, and the site is now part of the Tycho Brahe Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uraniborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Holberg, born in Bergen, wrote comedies and histories that founded modern Danish-Norwegian literature, and the astronomer Tycho Brahe, supported by Frederick II at his observatory of <b>Uraniborg</b> on Hven, made the most precise naked-eye observations of his age; Ole Rømer first measured the speed of light in 1676.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tycho Brahe’s observatory of <b>Uraniborg</b> on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II in 1576, produced the observations from which Johannes Kepler derived his laws of planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From <b>Uraniborg</b>, his observatory on the island of Hven, he made the most accurate observations recorded before the telescope.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
